 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Smooth Vertex Lighting with Ambient Occlusion Effect](10956-smooth-vertex-lighting-with-ambient-occlusion-effect.md)
-This method calculates the lighting value for a specific vertex of a polygon mesh generated from voxel data by averaging the light intensities of the four surrounding voxel cells in the direction of the face normal. It solves the problem of harsh, blocky lighting transitions and incorrect zero-light values on blocked faces by sampling neighbors rather than the cell itself. Unlike standard ray-casted ambient occlusion, it achieves a similar darkening effect in concave areas as a side effect of this averaging process, without the computational cost of ray casting. See [Smooth Vertex Lighting with Ambient Occlusion Effect](10956-smooth-vertex-lighting-with-ambient-occlusion-effect.md).
+This method calculates the lighting value for a specific vertex of a polygon mesh generated from voxel data by averaging the light intensities of the four surrounding voxel cells in the direction of the face normal. It solves the problem of harsh, blocky lighting transitions and incorrect zero-light values on blocked faces by sampling neighbors rather than the cell itself. Unlike standard ray-casted ambient occlusion, it achieves a similar darkening effect in concave areas as a side effect of this averaging process, without the computational cost of ray casting. It is chosen when smoother lighting gradients are required compared to the binary neighbor counting of the base method. See [Smooth Vertex Lighting with Ambient Occlusion Effect](10956-smooth-vertex-lighting-with-ambient-occlusion-effect.md).
 
 ### Least-Square Baking
-A method using signal processing to improve interpolated ambient occlusion factors by minimizing the distance between samples and interpolated data in the least-square sense [S3, unofficial]. It samples the occlusion signal uniformly across the mesh and derives vertex values to minimize the error between samples and interpolated data [S3, unofficial]. Developed for vertex data storage but can be applied to texture and volumes [S3, unofficial].
+A method using signal processing to improve interpolated ambient occlusion factors by minimizing the distance between samples and interpolated data in the least-square sense [S3, unofficial]. It samples the occlusion signal uniformly across the mesh and derives vertex values to minimize interpolation error [S3, unofficial]. Developed for vertex data storage but applicable to textures and volumes [S3, unofficial]. It is chosen when high-quality precomputed AO is needed for smooth interpolation across large meshes, at the cost of a more complex baking process.
 
 ### Blocky Ambient Occlusion Derivation
-Computes expected vertex AO purely from local geometry to replace hand-written offset tables, ensuring correctness against occluder positions [S4, unofficial]. For each vertex, it determines the face normal $\mathbf{n}$ and two in-plane steps $\mathbf{e}_1, \mathbf{e}_2$ pointing toward the vertex, then identifies occluder positions at $\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{e}_1$, $\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{e}_2$, and $\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{e}_1+\mathbf{e}_2$ [S4, unofficial]. The formula is $AO = 0.4$ if both sides are occluded; otherwise $AO = 1 - 0.2 \times \text{count\_of\_occluders}$ [S4, unofficial]. It replaces previous hand-written offset triples that required reordering when coordinate fixes were applied [S4, unofficial].
+Computes expected vertex AO purely from local geometry to replace hand-written offset tables, ensuring correctness against occluder positions [S4, unofficial]. For each vertex, it determines the face normal $\mathbf{n}$ and two in-plane steps $\mathbf{e}_1, \mathbf{e}_2$ pointing toward the vertex, then identifies occluder positions at $\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{e}_1$, $\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{e}_2$, and $\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{e}_1+\mathbf{e}_2$ [S4, unofficial]. The formula is $AO = 0.4$ if both sides are occluded; otherwise $AO = 1 - 0.2 \times \text{count\_of\_occluders}$ [S4, unofficial]. It replaces previous hand-written offset triples and is chosen for its robustness against meshing artifacts and offset table errors in blocky voxel engines.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-Time complexity is $O(1)$ per vertex, as it always checks a fixed 26-neighbor neighborhood. Memory complexity is $O(1)$ auxiliary space. The method is computationally inexpensive compared to ray casting, making it suitable for real-time applications [S2, unofficial]. However, the approximation may not accurately represent complex shadowing patterns compared to ray-traced methods. The quality depends on the voxel resolution and the accuracy of the vertex normals.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per vertex, as it always checks a fixed 26-neighbor neighborhood.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space per vertex.
+*   **Performance:** Very fast, suitable for real-time applications. The cost is dominated by the voxel grid access and basic arithmetic [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, no ray casting required, provides immediate visual feedback for crevices and concavities [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Approximate; can suffer from blocky artifacts if the mesh resolution is low or normals are inaccurate [S2, S4, unofficial]. The occlusion effect is limited to the immediate 1-voxel neighborhood.
+*   **When to Use:** Real-time rendering of voxel-based scenes where performance is critical and high-fidelity global illumination is not required.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When smooth, continuous lighting gradients are essential (use Smooth Vertex Lighting or Least-Square Baking), or when long-range occlusion is needed.
 
 ## Background
-The method is described in the context of voxel-based lighting in game engines, specifically in the Prometheus Engine [S2, unofficial] and Bevy Voxel Engine [S1, unofficial]. It is a simple approximation used to enhance visual quality in voxel-based rendering by simulating ambient occlusion effects at the vertex level.
+The method originates from the need to add shading detail to voxel-based meshes without the overhead of ray tracing. It is part of the family of voxel-based neighbor counting techniques. The specific "Vertex Ambient Occlusion Approximation" as defined by the neighbor-counting heuristic is documented in the Prometheus Engine implementation [S2, unofficial]. Variations and improvements, such as Least-Square Baking, have been explored in academic literature on real-time voxel global illumination [S3, unofficial]. The "Blocky AO" derivation was developed to fix inconsistencies in meshing pipelines, specifically replacing hand-tuned offset tables with a geometric derivation [S4, unofficial].
 
